<article class="grid max-w-5xl gap-6">
    {{ template "hero.gohtml" . }}

<pre><code>go get github.com/donseba/go-importmap@v1.5.0</code></pre>
<p>These examples target v1.5.0. They pin asset versions explicitly; the HTMX version below is an example dependency, not an instruction to resolve “latest”.</p>
<h2>A complete preparation command</h2>
<pre><code>package main

import (
	"context"
	"fmt"
	"log"
	"time"

	"github.com/donseba/go-importmap"
	"github.com/donseba/go-importmap/library"
)

func main() {
	ctx, cancel := context.WithTimeout(context.Background(), time.Minute)
	defer cancel()
	im := importmap.NewDefaults().WithPackages([]library.Package{{"{{"}}
		Name: "htmx", Version: "4.0.0",
		Require: library.Includes{{"{{"}}File: "htmx.esm.min.js", As: "htmx"}},
	}})
	if err := im.CacheOrFetch(ctx); err != nil {
		log.Fatal(err)
	}
	head, err := im.Render()
	if err != nil {
		log.Fatal(err)
	}
	fmt.Println(head)
}</code></pre>
<p>Run it in the application’s asset preparation directory. It creates cache and asset files and prints an import map pointing to <code>/assets/htmx/htmx.esm.min.js</code>.</p>
<p>This fetches at start-up, which suits development. For production, <a href="{{ basePath }}/vendoring" hx-get="{{ basePath }}/vendoring" hx-target="#content" hx-push-url="true">vendor the packages at build time</a> and embed them, so the program needs no network.</p>
<h2>Native module support</h2>
<p><code>NewDefaults</code> loads no module shim; this example relies on browsers with native import-map support. If your browser policy needs a shim, pass the URL of a vendored copy of <code>es-module-shims</code> to <code>ShimPath</code>.</p>
<p>Next, <a href="{{ basePath }}/serving" hx-get="{{ basePath }}/serving" hx-target="#content" hx-push-url="true">serve the generated directory and import the module</a>. Keep fetching out of the page-request path.</p>
</article>
